/*
 * Give uart0 (the header-pin UART, GPIO43/44 -- separate from the
 * native-USB console/shell on &usb_serial) a second interactive shell
 * instance, doubling as its log backend. uart0 is already `status = "okay"`
 * on this board and otherwise unused by the default v4-unified profile
 * (it's only claimed by CONFIG_MESHTASTIC_SERIAL / overlay-serial-local.conf
 * -- the netserial variant -- which is NOT part of this build; this
 * board-scoped overlay applies regardless of variant, so a V4 netserial
 * build would need to disable CONFIG_MESHTASTIC_SHELL_UART0 to avoid two
 * consumers fighting over the same physical UART).
 *
 * Added 2026-08-09: the native USB-CDC console has proven unreliable for
 * capturing early boot output under WSL2's USB passthrough (drops under
 * load, and cat/pyserial sessions have intermittently lost the connection
 * mid-boot) -- see docs/KNOWN-ISSUES.md and the friction log. A dedicated
 * FTDI-TTL cable on the uart0 header pins sidesteps all of that.
 *
 * First cut (same day) used a log-only backend (CONFIG_LOG_BACKEND_UART +
 * a `zephyr,log-uart` chosen node) instead of a shell. Replaced by this
 * shell instance because a real interactive shell was wanted here too, and
 * a plain log backend can't share the peripheral with one: log_backend_uart.c
 * writes via uart_poll_out() (synchronous polling), while the shell
 * transport (shell_uart.c) drives the same UART via uart_irq_tx_enable() /
 * uart_fifo_fill() (interrupt-driven) -- mixing the two access patterns on
 * one physical UART is not something Zephyr's driver model arbitrates
 * safely. A shell instance's own log_backend=true parameter (see
 * meshtastic_shell_uart0.c) already gives full log output through the same,
 * single, interrupt-driven owner, so the separate log-only backend is gone.
 */

/ {
	chosen {
		zephyr,meshtastic-shell-uart0 = &uart0;
	};
};
